    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d69">

                <pb facs="0123" n="109" sameAs="frus:frus1948v05p1/pg109"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.20 Missions/2–1648: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Iran</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Allen</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-02-16T17:00:00+03:30">February 16,
                            1948—5 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>189. During my farewell call<anchor n="1" xml:id="d69-f1"/> on Prime Minister
                    today I expressed concurrence in Ambassador Ala’s recommendation (Dept’s 131,
                        <pb facs="0124" n="110" xml:id="pg110"/> February 10) that Iran Government
                    submit to UN Soviet note of January 31 and Iranian reply. Prime Minister said he
                    agreed but asked whether I thought Soviets would be seriously annoyed by such
                    action. I said they would undoubtedly be displeased but that any disadvantage on
                    this score would be more than offset by advantage of letting Soviets and world
                    know that Iran had confidence in UN and that Soviets could not expect to write
                    notes of this kind without having them brought to attention of world
                    organization. I said that for action to be effective it must have no appearance
                    of having been prompted.</p>
                <p>Hakimi gave every indication of intention to send notes to Secretary General for
                        SC information.</p>
                <p>Sent Department as 189, repeated London as 20.</p>
                <p>Department pass Moscow as 12.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Allen</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d69-f1" n="1" xml:id="d69-f1-content">
                <p>Ambassador Allen was returning to Washington to serve as Assistant Secretary of
                    State for Public Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
